ingle L2 bridge domain\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"node1's eth1 and eth2 were placed under a common Linux bridge (br0) with STP disabled; both ports reached 'forwarding' state, confirmed both before and after node1's restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-63b1053b58354762\",\"cmd-0fe60f1b59004cdc\",\"cmd-9994385abbc64236\",\"cmd-0bc29df373be420a\",\"cmd-1fc1b12739774663\",\"cmd-588b1fd21d484b4a\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 share a single subnet\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Static IPs 192.168.50.11/24 (node2) and 192.168.50.12/24 (node3) were assigned on eth1 via nmcli with autoconnect enabled, verified applied.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9f18dee0572f4971\",\"cmd-d1c27a52962d4904\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"node2 and node3 reach each other directly with node1 forwarding frames at L2, not routing\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Ping and arping between the two hosts succeeded showing the peer's real MAC address (not node1's), node1's bridge FDB shows both peer MACs learned on their correct ingress ports, and br0 carries no IPv4 address, confirming pure L2 forwarding. Reconfirmed after all three restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-d4283a2d7bd546d9\",\"cmd-e4b189f08a584819\",\"cmd-9d07950f1cf8467c\",\"cmd-0e8c69f2f2f04d6b\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"Bridge configuration persists across a node1 reboot rather than being only a transient runtime state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd oneshot unit (l2-bridge.service) was created and enabled, interfaces-config.service was masked to prevent reconciliation from reverting the manual bridge, and after a full node1 reboot the bridge, forwarding state, FDB entries, and end-to-end connectivity were directly observed to be restored automatically.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-bb374581b8234cd4\",\"cmd-ab743f742fdb476d\",\"cmd-b8de1143a869458c\",\"cmd-588b1fd21d484b4a\",\"cmd-9d07950f1cf8467c\"]}],\"confidence\":0.88,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.75,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands map cleanly to the task or the restart protocol. Two exceptions: (1) a native SONiC 'config vlan add 100' / VLAN-member experiment (cmd-cf71a2cfc88c4044, cmd-94b58a98f89b445a) was attempted, failed because eth1/eth2 are unbound virtio ports absent from the SONiC PORT table, and was fully reverted by recreating br0 from scratch (cmd-1fc1b12739774663) — a bounded, reverted troubleshooting detour with no lasting residue. (2) Removing docker0's stray class-E address and masking interfaces-config.service (cmd-ee012b57ddd044ed, cmd-ab743f742fdb476d) are broader, retained system changes; they are plausibly necessary to stop SONiC's config-reconciliation from reverting the manual bridge, but their causal necessity for docker0 specifically is not directly demonstrated. Post-restart snapshot for node1 also shows a newly-failed 'system-health.service' that was not failed at baseline, which is not explained by any executor action and should be treated as an unexplained residual rather than attributed to the task.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-cf71a2cfc88c4044\",\"cmd-94b58a98f89b445a\",\"cmd-1fc1b12739774663\",\"cmd-ee012b57ddd044ed\",\"cmd-ab743f742fdb476d\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node1:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The executor built a Linux bridge (br0) on node1 joining eth1 and eth2, assigned node2 and node3 addresses in a shared 192.168.50.0/24 subnet, and directly demonstrated L2-only forwarding (no IP on br0, correct FDB learning of peer MACs, and ping/arping showing the true peer MAC rather than node1's). Persistence was implemented via a systemd unit and masking of the config-reconciliation service, and this was validated end-to-end by rebooting all three nodes in turn and observing changed boot identities alongside fully recovered bridge, FDB, and connectivity state, corroborated by the fixed before/after lifecycle snapshots. All material outcomes of the task are satisfied with direct evidence.\",\"limitations\":[\"A brief, fully-reverted SONiC 'config vlan' experiment occurred mid-task; it left no residue but represents an unnecessary mutation during troubleshooting.\",\"The docker0 IP cleanup and masking of interfaces-config.service are retained system changes whose strict necessity for the bridging outcome is inferred from the executor's narrative rather than independently proven.\",\"A new failed unit (system-health.service) appears on node1 only in the after-executor snapshot; the trace does not explain whether this is caused by the executor's changes or is incidental to the restart, which is a gap in the evidence.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Join the two segments meeting at node1 (eth1/eth2) into a single L2 bridge domain","status":"satisfied","summary":"node1's eth1 and eth2 were placed under a common Linux bridge (br0) with STP disabled; both ports reached 'forwarding' state, confirmed both before and after node1's restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-63b1053b58354762","cmd-0fe60f1b59004cdc","cmd-9994385abbc64236","cmd-0bc29df373be420a","cmd-1fc1b12739774663","cmd-588b1fd21d484b4a"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"node2 and node3 share a single subnet","status":"satisfied","summary":"Static IPs 192.168.50.11/24 (node2) and 192.168.50.12/24 (node3) were assigned on eth1 via nmcli with autoconnect enabled, verified applied.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9f18dee0572f4971","cmd-d1c27a52962d4904"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"node2 and node3 reach each other directly with node1 forwarding frames at L2, not routing","status":"satisfied","summary":"Ping and arping between the two hosts succeeded showing the peer's real MAC address (not node1's), node1's bridge FDB shows both peer MACs learned on their correct ingress ports, and br0 carries no IPv4 address, confirming pure L2 forwarding. Reconfirmed after all three restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4283a2d7bd546d9","cmd-e4b189f08a584819","cmd-9d07950f1cf8467